{"groupId":"G1.8","requirement":"Data mismatches and unsupported figures within the report itself — Consistency: Where client and family facts and financial information have been included in the Suitability Report, are they consistent throughout the suitability report (including facts used in all narrative sections, calculations, cashflow modelling, projections and assumptions) and with all supporting evidence files provided?","reportSays":"The extraction identified four internal inconsistencies within the report itself: (1) Expenditure figures differ between sections — the narrative states £2,493 essential + £800 discretionary + £417 holidays = £3,710 per month, but the Cashflow Analysis section states £2,206 per month (a discrepancy of approximately £1,387 or 63%). (2) Platform charge reduction stated inconsistently — the Existing Arrangements table shows 0.32% platform + 0.22% fund = 0.54% total, the recommendation section states a reduction from 0.32% to 0.21%, but the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table shows the impact as +0.08% (resulting in 0.62%, not 0.21%), with fund charges changing from 0.22% to 0.41%. (3) Recommended fund allocation differs between sections — the Recommendations section states 'Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II (100%)' for Pension, but the ISA & GIA section shows 'CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc (100%)', both at 100% allocations to different funds. (4) Adviser fee calculation inconsistency — the 'Our charges' section states an initial fee of 3% of £159,812 = £4,794, but the Cashflow Analysis section states the GIA investment is '£57,120 (minus the initial fee of £4,794.37)', suggesting the fee is deducted from the GIA, leaving £52,325.63 to invest, which would result in a total investment value of £155,812 (not £159,812) and an ongoing fee of 1% of £155,812 = £1,558.12.","fileSays":"The report itself contains these four internal inconsistencies as identified by the extraction. No external supporting documents are provided to corroborate or contradict these figures. The comparison is internal to the report only. Additionally, the derived figures computed from the report's own extracted values show that: (a) A charge 0.08% higher cannot produce a reduction in yield 0.1% lower (High growth scenario: 1.8% vs 1.7%), identical (Low and Mid growth scenarios: both 1.6% and 1.7%), or zero change (all three scenarios state £0.00 / 0% effect of switching) on the same basis. The report does not reconcile these contradictions between the stated annual charge difference and the projected yield differences.","discrepancies":"1. Expenditure: Narrative section states £2,493 + £800 + £417 (holidays) = £3,710 per month; Cashflow Analysis section states £2,206 per month — a difference of £1,504 per month (or 68% if calculated as (3710-2206)/2206). 2. Platform charges: Existing Arrangements table shows 0.54% total (0.32% platform + 0.22% fund); recommendation narrative states platform charge reduces from 0.32% to 0.21%; Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table shows +0.08% impact (0.54% to 0.62%) with fund charges changing from 0.22% to 0.41%. 3. Recommended funds: Pension section states 'Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II (100%)'; ISA & GIA section states 'CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc (100%)' — two different funds both at 100%. 4. Adviser fee basis: 'Our charges' section calculates 3% of £159,812 = £4,794; Cashflow Analysis section deducts £4,794.37 from £57,120 GIA, leaving £52,325.63, implying total investment of £155,812 (not £159,812). 5. Switching cost vs yield impact: Report states recommended option costs 0.08% more annually but projects identical or better yield outcomes (High growth: 1.8% vs 1.7%; Low/Mid growth: 1.6%/1.7% vs 1.6%/1.7%; all scenarios: £0.00 / 0% effect). A higher annual charge cannot produce identical or better yields on the same basis.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"The report does not state the assumptions underlying the yield comparisons (High/Low/Mid growth scenarios) or explain what adjustments, if any, were applied to each side of the comparison to produce identical or improved yields despite a 0.08% higher annual charge. The basis for the fund charge increase from 0.22% to 0.41% is not explained. The reconciliation between the fee calculation basis (£159,812 vs £155,812) is not clarified in the report.","analysis":"Four material internal inconsistencies are present in the report: (1) Expenditure discrepancy: The narrative describes £2,493 essential + £800 discretionary + £417 holidays (monthly equivalent) = £3,710 per month total, but the Cashflow Analysis section states £2,206 per month. This is a 68% difference and material to affordability assessment and cashflow projections. The extraction notes this is marked as a consolidated figure for cashflow modelling purposes, but the report does not explain why the two figures differ or which is used for which purpose. (2) Charge inconsistency: The report states the platform charge reduces from 0.32% to 0.21% (a saving of 0.11%), but simultaneously states the total charge impact is +0.08% (from 0.54% to 0.62%). This is internally contradictory. The fund charge also changes from 0.22% to 0.41% (an increase of 0.19%), which more than offsets the stated platform saving. The report does not reconcile these figures or explain the net effect. (3) Fund allocation inconsistency: Two different funds are recommended at 100% allocation each — one for the Pension and one for ISA & GIA. The report does not clarify whether this is intentional (different funds for different wrappers) or an error. The narrative in the Recommended Solution section appears to describe a single multi-asset fund, creating ambiguity. (4) Fee calculation basis: The initial fee is calculated as 3% of £159,812 = £4,794, but the Cashflow Analysis deducts this fee from the GIA investment (£57,120 - £4,794.37 = £52,325.63), implying the total amount invested is £155,812 (not £159,812). This affects the ongoing fee calculation (1% of £155,812 = £1,558.12 vs 1% of £159,812 = £1,598.12). The report does not clarify whether the fee is deducted from the GIA or paid separately. (5) Switching cost paradox: The derived figures show the report states a 0.08% annual charge increase but projects identical or improved yields in all three growth scenarios (High growth: 1.8% vs 1.7% reduction; Low/Mid growth: 1.6%/1.7% vs 1.6%/1.7% reduction; all scenarios: £0.00 / 0% effect). This is mathematically inconsistent on the same basis. The report does not explain what assumptions or adjustments produce this outcome. These are not rounding differences or presentation variations — they are material contradictions on figures that drive the recommendation (charges, fees, expenditure, fund selection).","citations":[{"passageId":"I_extractionReport_internalInconsistencies_0","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.pdf (extraction metadata)","category":"I","quote":"Expenditure figures differ between sections. The 'Your current situation' section states 'Your current regular expenditure is approximately £2,493 per month covering essential costs such as household bills, transport, and food. You also have discretionary spending of approximately £800 per month on entertainment and leisure, plus around £5,000 annually on holidays.' This totals approximately £3,593 per month (£2,493 + £800 + £417 for holidays). However, the Cashflow Analysis section states 'Your current expenditure is approximately £2,206 per month.' This is a discrepancy of approximately £1,387 per month or 63% difference.","cells":null},{"passageId":"I_extractionReport_internalInconsistencies_1","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.pdf (extraction metadata)","category":"I","quote":"Platform charge reduction stated inconsistently. The Existing Arrangements table shows current charges as '0.32% platform charge + 0.22% weighted fund charges' (total 0.54%). The recommendation section states 'By moving the pension under TFAS agency, you will benefit from a reduced platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%.' However, the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table shows 'Current Annual Recurring Charge of Existing Plan: 0.54%' and 'Impact of Recommended Alternative: +0.08%' which would result in 0.62%, not the 0.21% platform charge stated elsewhere. The fund charge also appears to change from 0.22% to 0.41% in the recommended solution.","cells":null},{"passageId":"I_extractionReport_internalInconsistencies_2","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.pdf (extraction metadata)","category":"I","quote":"Recommended fund allocation differs between sections. The Recommendations section heading states 'Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II (100%)' for the Pension. However, the Fund recommendation table later shows 'Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II' at 100% for Pension, but the ISA & GIA section shows 'CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc' at 100%. The narrative in 'Recommended Solution - Pension' section appears to describe a single multi-asset fund, but the structure suggests different funds for different wrappers.","cells":null},{"passageId":"I_extractionReport_internalInconsistencies_3","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.pdf (extraction metadata)","category":"I","quote":"Adviser fee calculation inconsistency. The 'Our charges' section states 'For researching, recommending and arranging these investments, we charge an initial advice fee of 3% of the amount invested... In monetary terms this is £4,794 based on total investment values (£79,092 pension transfer + £57,120 GIA + £20,000 ISA + £3,600 pension contribution).' This totals £159,812, and 3% of this is £4,794.36, which matches. However, the Cashflow Analysis section states the GIA investment is '£57,120 (minus the initial fee of £4,794.37)' which suggests the fee is deducted from the GIA, leaving £52,325.63 to invest. The total investment value used in the cashflow appears to be £155,812 (£79,092 + £52,325.63 + £20,000 + £3,600), not £159,812.","cells":null},{"passageId":"I_derivedFigures_switchingCost","source":"Derived figures computed from canonical model","category":"I","quote":"The report states the recommended option costs 0.08% a year more than the existing one, and reports a reduction in yield of 1.8% for the existing plan against 1.7% for the recommended one at High growth. A charge 0.08% higher cannot produce a reduction in yield 0.1% lower than the plan it replaces on the same basis.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Missing component"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

