hich any surplus income is held. This is also the first liquid asset to be drawn from if there is a shortfall. A Required withdrawal (Investments) is made from available liquid assets when there is a shortfall in a year (where expenditure is greater than income). A Required withdrawal (Pensions) is made from accessible pensions when there is a shortfall in a year and it cannot be met from liquid assets.\" The evidence does not explicitly quantify what the emergency fund should be, does not state whether the £10,000 is sufficient, and does not address whether the emergency fund is depleted by the recommendation or the planned £40,000 renovation spend.","discrepancies":"1. The report asserts the emergency fund is \"funded from Inheritance\" but does not clarify whether the £10,000 emergency fund is part of the £100,000 inheritance or separate from it. If part of the inheritance, the total liquid inheritance available is £100,000, of which £40,000 is earmarked for renovations and £10,000 for emergency reserves, leaving only £50,000 for investment (not the £60,000 stated as the amount to be invested). 2. The report states the client \"intends to spend circa £40,000 of your inheritance on renovations\" and \"would like to look at investing the remaining £60,000,\" but does not reconcile this with the £10,000 emergency fund. If the emergency fund is part of the inheritance, the arithmetic is: £100,000 - £40,000 (renovations) - £10,000 (emergency) = £50,000 available for investment, not £60,000. 3. The report asserts affordability based on the initial fee being \"comfortably affordable from your inheritance funds without affecting your current lifestyle,\" but the inheritance is already committed to £40,000 renovations and £10,000 emergency reserves, leaving only £50,000 (or £60,000 if the emergency fund is separate). The fee of £4,794 would further reduce the amount available for investment. 4. The report does not state whether the emergency fund of £10,000 is adequate gi