rvice enhancements) that the report does not quantify or reconcile. \n\nThe RIY figures show: at High growth, the recommended plan's RIY is 0.1% lower (better) than the existing plan despite higher costs; at Mid and Low growth, RIY is identical despite higher costs. The report does not explain what assumption or benefit produces this outcome. The stated \"effect of switching\" as £0.00 / 0% across all scenarios directly contradicts the assertion of 0.08% annual cost increase.\n\nThe platform charge narrative (0.32% to 0.21%, a 0.11% reduction) conflicts with the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table showing +0.08% total impact. This implies fund charges rise from 0.22% to 0.41%, offsetting the platform saving. The report does not disclose or explain this fund charge increase or justify why it represents better value.\n\nThe adviser fee calculation shows internal inconsistency: the stated 3% of £159,812 = £4,794 is arithmetically correct, but the Cashflow Analysis treatment (fee deducted from GIA, leaving £52,325.63 to invest) implies a different total investment base of £155,812. This ambiguity affects whether the ongoing 1% fee (stated as £1,558) is correctly calculated and whether the total cost impact is fully disclosed.\n\nThe report claims benefits including consolidation, oversight, and flexibility, but does not quantify these or demonstrate they justify the 0.08% annual cost increase. The stated benefits are qualitative and not reconciled against the cost comparison. The requirement to explain benefits expected in exchange for higher costs is not met: the report asserts benefits but does not show they outweigh the additional 0.08% annual charge, particularly given the contradictory RIY and switching effect figures.\n\nThe extraction metadata notes that the report contains internal inconsistencies on platform charges and adviser fee calculations, confirming these are not extraction errors but genuine contradictions in the source document.","citations":[