gy 60% at 35.02% + LifeStrategy 80% at 64.95%)","Reconciliation of the platform charge reduction claim (0.32% to 0.21%) with the fund charge stated in the Aviva factsheet (0.41%)","Explanation of how a 0.08% annual cost increase produces identical or lower RIY on the same basis","Explanation of how the switching effect can be £0.00 / 0% at all growth rates when the annual charge difference is 0.08%","Basis for the claim that the recommended fund's performance is 'achievable' given it has underperformed the existing portfolio over 5 years"],"analysis":"The report asserts that the recommendation is justified by benefits including lower costs (platform charge reduction from 0.32% to 0.21%), improved risk alignment, and professional oversight. However, multiple material inconsistencies undermine this justification. First, the claimed platform charge reduction to 0.21% contradicts the Aviva factsheet [P12], which states the fund charge is 0.41% (AMC 0.30% + Additional Expenses 0.11%). The report's own extracted data shows the total ongoing charge increases from 0.54% to 0.62% (a 0.08% increase), not a decrease. Second, the risk alignment benefit is undermined by ambiguity: the report claims existing funds have \"risk level of 6,\" but the holdings shown in [P10] and [P15] are Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% Equity (typically risk level 5) and LifeStrategy 80% Equity (risk level 6), creating a blended portfolio whose actual risk level is unclear. More critically, the recommended Aviva fund has underperformed the existing portfolio over 5 years (36.21% vs 41.83% [P3], [P2], [P8]), contradicting the claim that switching to a lower-risk fund will improve outcomes. Third, the report's own derived figures reveal a fundamental inconsistency: it states the recommended option costs 0.08% more annually, yet claims the RIY is 1.8% for existing vs 1.7% for recommended at High growth, and identical (1.6%) at Low and Mid growth. A charge 0.08% higher cannot produce lower or identical RIY on