{"groupId":"G7.1","requirement":"All material costs disclosed — product, platform, fund, transaction, advice. Limb: Consistency","reportSays":"The report discloses platform charges of 0.21% for Pension, ISA and GIA; investment management charges of 0.41% for Pension (Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II), 0.29% for ISA (CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc), and 0.29% for GIA (CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc). Total ongoing charges are stated as 0.62% for Pension, 0.50% for ISA, and 0.50% for GIA. The report states an initial advice fee of 3% (£4,794) and ongoing adviser fee of 1% per annum. Transaction costs are excluded with the stated reason that they are variable and already accounted for in fund performance figures presented net of all charges. The report states the recommended option costs 0.08% more annually than the existing plan (0.62% vs 0.54%), and that this increase is justified by improved risk alignment and ongoing professional oversight.","fileSays":"Existing arrangements show platform charge of 0.32% and weighted fund charges of 0.22%, totalling 0.54% [P21]. The Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II factsheet [P4] does not specify the fund charge percentage. The Aviva Pension Illustration [P6] shows the Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II S14 at 0.42% OCF/TER charge. The CT Universal MAP Cautious C Acc KIID [P7] shows ongoing charges of 0.29%. The GIA Illustration [P8] shows CT Universal MAP Cautious C Acc at 0.29%. The Pension projection documents [P1, P2, P3] show existing holdings (Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% Equity and 80% Equity) with weighted average charge of 0.22%. The GIA research report [P14] shows CT Universal MAP Cautious C Acc ongoing charges of 0.29% and platform charge of 0.21%. The Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment [P21] confirms existing plan at 0.54% and impact of +0.08%. The GIA research report [P24] shows CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc with ongoing costs of 0.29%, transaction costs of 0.22%, totalling 0.51% when transaction costs are included. The Aviva GIA Illustration [P19, P20] shows cumulative effect of charges reducing investment growth from 4.5% to 2.0% (a 2.5% reduction) when all charges are included.","discrepancies":"\n1. Investment management charge for recommended Pension fund: Report states 0.41% for Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II, but Aviva Pension Illustration [P6] shows 0.42% for the same fund (Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II S14 GB00B8BV3X17).\n2. Transaction costs disclosure: Report excludes transaction costs stating they are variable and already accounted for in fund performance net of all charges. However, GIA research report [P24] separately itemises transaction costs of 0.22% for CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc, showing they are tracked and quantifiable, not merely variable unknowns.\n3. Cumulative cost impact not clearly reconciled: Report states recommended plan costs 0.08% more (0.62% vs 0.54%) but does not explain how this 0.08% increase reconciles with the derived figures showing that a charge 0.08% higher cannot produce identical or better RIY outcomes at Low, Mid and High growth scenarios (all showing 0% difference in effect of switching despite +0.08% annual cost).\n4. Adviser fee basis inconsistency: Report states initial fee of 3% of £159,812 = £4,794, but also states GIA investment is £57,120 minus initial fee of £4,794.37, leaving £52,325.63. The cashflow analysis appears to use £155,812 (after fee deduction) rather than £159,812 (before fee), affecting ongoing fee calculation basis.\n5. Platform charge reduction claim: Report states \"reduced platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%\" but the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment [P21] shows the impact as +0.08% overall, not a reduction. The fund charge appears to increase from 0.22% to 0.41%, offsetting the platform charge reduction.\n","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"\n1. Explicit reconciliation of how a 0.08% annual cost increase produces zero change in projected outcomes at Low, Mid and High growth scenarios.\n2. Clarification of whether the initial adviser fee of £4,794 is deducted from the investment amounts before calculating ongoing fees, and if so, what the correct basis for ongoing fee calculation is.\n3. Explanation of why transaction costs are excluded from the report's cost disclosure when they are separately quantified in the GIA research report at 0.22%.\n","analysis":"The report discloses all material cost layers (platform, investment management, initial advice, ongoing advice) and states transaction costs are excluded with reasons. However, several inconsistencies emerge when comparing the report against supporting evidence.\n\nFirst, the investment management charge for the recommended Pension fund is stated as 0.41% in the report but the Aviva Pension Illustration [P6] shows 0.42% for the same fund. This is a 0.01% discrepancy.\n\nSecond, the transaction costs exclusion is stated as justified because they are \"variable and subject to change\" and \"already accounted for in the fund's performance figures, which are presented net of all charges.\" However, the GIA research report [P24] separately itemises transaction costs of 0.22% for the CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc, demonstrating that transaction costs are not merely variable unknowns but are tracked and quantifiable. The report's exclusion is therefore not fully justified by the stated reasoning — transaction costs are identifiable and material (0.22% for the GIA fund).\n\nThird, the derived figures identify a fundamental inconsistency: the report states the recommended option costs 0.08% more annually than the existing plan, yet reports RIY reductions that are identical or better for the recommended plan at Low, Mid and High growth scenarios. Specifically:\n- At High growth: existing RIY 1.8%, recommended RIY 1.7% (0.1% better despite +0.08% cost)\n- At Mid growth: existing RIY 1.7%, recommended RIY 1.7% (identical despite +0.08% cost)\n- At Low growth: existing RIY 1.6%, recommended RIY 1.6% (identical despite +0.08% cost)\n- Effect of switching at all three growth scenarios: £0.00 / 0%\n\nA charge 0.08% higher cannot produce identical or better RIY outcomes on the same basis. The report does not reconcile these two statements or explain what different assumptions (e.g., different fund performance, different time horizons, different fee structures) were applied to each side of the comparison.\n\nFourth, the adviser fee calculation shows internal inconsistency. The report states the initial fee is 3% of £159,812 (total investment values) = £4,794. However, the report also states the GIA investment is \"£57,120 (minus the initial fee of £4,794.37)\" leaving £52,325.63 to invest. This suggests the fee is deducted from the investment amount. If so, the total amount actually invested would be £155,812 (£79,092 + £52,325.63 + £20,000 + £3,600), not £159,812. The ongoing fee calculation basis is therefore unclear — is it 1% of £159,812 or 1% of £155,812?\n\nFifth, the platform charge reduction claim requires clarification. The report states \"By moving the pension under TFAS agency, you will benefit from a reduced platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%.\" However, the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment [P21] shows the overall impact as +0.08%, not a reduction. The fund charge increases from 0.22% to 0.41%, offsetting the platform charge reduction from 0.32% to 0.21%. The report's statement about platform charge reduction is accurate but incomplete — it does not disclose that the fund charge increases, resulting in a net cost increase of 0.08%.\n\nThe cumulative effect of charges is disclosed in the GIA Illustration [P19, P20], which shows that all charges could reduce investment growth from 4.5% to 2.0% (a 2.5% reduction in growth rate). This is a material disclosure of cumulative impact. However, the report does not reference this cumulative effect illustration in its own cost disclosure section, creating a gap between the report's cost narrative and the supporting illustrations.\n\nThe report's exclusion of transaction costs with stated reasons is a disclosure (not an omission), but the justification is weakened by evidence that transaction costs are quantifiable (0.22% for the GIA fund) and material. The report should have either included transaction costs or provided a more robust explanation of why they are excluded despite being identifiable in the supporting research.\n\nThe comparison can be performed because the evidence provides charge figures for both existing and recommended arrangements. However, the reconciliation of the cost increase against the projected outcomes cannot be completed from the evidence provided — the report does not explain the basis for the identical RIY outcomes despite higher costs.","citations":[{"passageId":"P6","source":"Aviva - Pension Illustration.md","category":"H","quote":"Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II S14 GB00B8BV3X17 | 0.42%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"Aviva Pension Portfolio - AV2075926-001 | 0.54% | £427 | +0.08% | +£63","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"GIA research report 03-12-2025 15-40.md","category":"G","quote":"CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc | - | £174.00 (0.29%) | £133.26 (0.22%) | - | £307.26 (0.51%)","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"The charges quoted in this report exclude the transaction costs of the underlying funds, as these are variable and subject to change based on factors such as market conditions and trading activity. Transaction costs are not fees paid to the asset manager; instead, they include expenses like broker commissions, taxes, and the cost of executing trades. Despite their variability, these costs are already accounted for in the fund's performance figures, which are presented net of all charges.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Aviva - GIA Illustration.md","category":"H","quote":"All charges could reduce the value of this Investment Portfolio from £88,700 to £69,900 - this is the same as reducing the investment growth after inflation from 4.5% to 2.0%. This is a reduction in the growth rate of 2.5%.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"CT Universal MAP Cautious C Acc - KIID.md","category":"H","quote":"Ongoing Charges: 0.29%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Aviva - GIA Illustration.md","category":"H","quote":"CT Universal MAP Cautious C Acc | 0.29%","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Disclosure shortfall","Weak justification"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

