educe \"from 0.32% to 0.21%\".","discrepancies":"1. Platform charge reduction claim contradicts the overall cost comparison: The report states the platform charge reduces from 0.32% to 0.21% (a saving of 0.11%), yet the total charge increases by 0.08%. This is only possible if the fund charge increases by 0.19% (from 0.22% to 0.41%), which the report does disclose in [P7] but does not explain in the narrative justification [P15]. The narrative emphasizes the platform charge reduction without highlighting the larger fund charge increase.\n\n2. Existing plan charges stated inconsistently across tables: The Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment [P5] shows the existing plan at 0.54% (£427), but the pension switch research report [P8] and [P14] show the existing solution at 0.6% in the new solution charges comparison table. This is a 0.06% discrepancy (or approximately £47 on the £79,092 fund value).\n\n3. Recommended plan charges stated inconsistently: The Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment [P5] shows the impact as +0.08%, implying recommended charges of 0.62%. The pension switch research report [P8] and [P14] show the new solution at 0.7%, which is 0.08% higher than the 0.62% stated in [P7]. This creates ambiguity about whether the comparison includes adviser fees or other charges not disclosed in the main suitability report.\n\n4. Effect of switching shows zero impact despite cost increase: The report states the recommended option costs 0.08% more annually, yet the critical yield analysis [P11] and the pension switch research report [P21] show the effect of switching at Low, Mid, and High growth rates as £0.00 / 0%. A recommendation that costs more every year cannot produce zero change in projected outcome on the same basis without additional assumptions (such as fund performance differences or different growth assumptions) being applied and disclosed.\n\n5. Basis of comparison not clearly disclosed: The report presents the \"Like for Like\" comparison at 0.08% additional cost but does not explicitly state whether this includes or excludes the 1% ongoing adviser fee. The statement that \"including our 1% annual fee, the total cost is approximately 1.62% annually compared to your current arrangement of 0.54%\" appears later but is not integrated into the main cost comparison table.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"1. The fund charge for the recommended plan (0.41%) is not explained or justified in the narrative. The report does not disclose why the fund charge increases from 0.22% to 0.41% or what fund is being recommended to justify this increase.\n\n2. The basis of the 0.6% and 0.7% figures in the pension switch research report [P8] and [P14] is not reconciled with the 0.54% and 0.62% figures in the main suitability report. It is unclear whether these represent different calculation methods, different dates, or inclusion/exclusion of adviser fees.\n\n3. The assumptions underlying the zero effect of switching (£0.00 / 0%) are not disclosed. The report does not explain what fund performance, growth rates, or other factors produce this outcome despite the stated 0.08% annual cost increase.","analysis":"The report contains multiple internal inconsistencies in its cost comparison that undermine the reliability of the conclusion that the recommended option is suitable despite higher costs.\n\nFirst, the platform charge reduction narrative is misleading. The report emphasizes that the platform charge will reduce from 0.32% to 0.21%, which is presented as a benefit in [P15]. However, this 0.11% saving is more than offset by a 0.19% increase in fund charges (from 0.22% to 0.41%), resulting in a net cost increase of 0.08%. The report discloses the fund charge increase in the charges table [P7] but does not explain it in the narrative justification, creating a disconnect between the stated benefits and the actual cost impact.\n\nSecond, the existing plan charge is stated as 0.54% in the main suitability report [P5] but as