ting versus recommended solutions. The reconciliation between the two different sets of RIY figures (those in [P15] versus [P4]) is not explained in the report.","analysis":"The requirement is to assess consistency of reduction in yield, critical yield and cumulative cost illustrations. Two material inconsistencies are evident: First, the RIY figures stated in the suitability report's Critical Yield table [P15] differ substantially from those in the Pension switch report [P4]. At Low growth, the existing pension RIY is stated as 1.6% in [P15] but 0.6% in [P4] — a 1.0 percentage point difference. At Mid growth, the existing is 1.7% in [P15] but 0.6% in [P4]. These are not rounding differences; they represent fundamentally different calculations. The report does not explain which figures are correct or why they differ. Second, the report's statement that the 1% adviser charge has been applied to \"both the new and old solution projections\" [P14] is contradicted by evidence that the existing plan has no current adviser charge [P16]. If the 1% charge was applied only to the new solution but not the existing, this would artificially inflate the RIY for the existing plan and suppress the apparent cost of switching. This is consistent with the finding in [P16] that loading a charge the client does not pay onto the existing plan produces the \"£0 or 0.0% decrease\" effect of switching. Third, the report's narrative statement that the recommended plan must outperform the current plan by approximately 1.08% annually [P16] is arithmetically inconsistent with the appendix showing an effect of switching of £0 or 0.0% at all growth rates [P15]. A recommendation costing 1.08% more annually cannot produce zero change in projected outcome on the same growth basis. The guard on different bases applies here: the body statement may rest on different assumptions (e.g., different inflation rates, different time horizons, or different adviser charge treatments) than the appendix, but the