assumptions used and actual experience can result in a marked difference in the outcome when compounded over a long period of time. Please also note that the chart does not consider any potential long-term care costs which could occur in later life\" (page 18).","fileSays":"The product literature (Aviva Key Features documents) discloses multiple material risks and disadvantages: (1) Capital risk: \"The value of the investments in your ISA Portfolio and any income from them may go down as well as up and may be worth less than the amount invested\" [P5]; \"The value of your pension pot and the retirement benefits it can provide may go down as well as up. You could get back less than has been invested\" [P10]; (2) Market timing and dealing risks: \"As a result of trading practices, there's a possibility that the price of investments may fall or rise between us receiving your instructions and the time of the transaction\" [P7]; (3) Emerging market risks: \"Investments in emerging or developing markets may carry higher risks as they may be subject to considerable fluctuations in value\" [P3, P7, P8]; (4) Small company risks: \"Small company investments may take longer to sell than those in larger companies, so the price fluctuations may be greater\" [P3, P7, P8]; (5) Currency/overseas investment risks: \"The value of any overseas investments will be influenced by the rate of exchange used to convert to sterling\" [P3, P7, P8]; (6) Property investment risks: \"If the underlying investments include property, it may take some time before we can sell your investment\" [P3, P7, P8]; (7) Pension transfer risks: \"If you're transferring benefits from an older company pension scheme it could be a Defined Benefit Pension and you may be losing valuable rights or benefits within the scheme such as a guaranteed income based on your final salary\" [P9]; \"There's no guarantee that what you receive at retirement will be greater than what you could have received from the previous scheme\" [P9]; (8) Charge impact risks: \"The price of some investments includes initial and yearly costs that will have an effect on your investments. Initial costs will have an immediate effect on the value of those investments\" [P10, P16]; (9) Disinvestment risks: \"If there isn't enough in your cash account to pay your income, your adviser will be able to choose on your behalf how to sell assets in order to do so (this is called 'disinvestment')\" [P10]; (10) Inflation risk: \"If investments grow less quickly than the rate of inflation, the buying power of your money will reduce\" [P5]; (11) ISA tax treatment risk: \"It's possible that the current favourable tax treatment of Individual Savings Accounts may change in the future\" [P7]; (12) Cancellation and market movement risk: \"If you cancel your investment within the cancellation period, and your payment was a single payment, you may not get back all of your original payment\" [P5, P10]; (13) Fund suspension/dealing restrictions: \"You may not be able to sell or switch between funds if the fund manager concerned has deferred or suspended dealing in the fund at that time\" [P7].","discrepancies":"[1] Pension transfer disadvantages: The product literature [P9] explicitly warns that transferring from a defined benefit pension may result in loss of valuable rights such as guaranteed income based on final salary, and states \"There's no guarantee that what you receive at retirement will be greater than what you could have received from the previous scheme.\" The report does not disclose that the client is transferring from an existing pension arrangement (Aviva Life & Pensions UK Ltd with Vanguard LifeStrategy funds) or what specific benefits or protections may be lost in that transfer. The extraction notes indicate that /replacementAnalysis/0/featuresOrGuaranteesLost is absent from the report. [2] Emerging market, small company, currency and property risks: The product literature [P3, P7, P8] contains specific warnings about emerging market investments, small company investments, currency/overseas investment risks, and property investment risks. The report does not identify whether the recommended Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II contains any of these asset classes or exposures, and therefore does not disclose these specific risks to the client. [3] Fund suspension and dealing restriction risks: The product literature [P7] warns \"You may not be able to sell or switch between funds if the fund manager concerned has deferred or suspended dealing in the fund at that time. This is more likely to occur during times of poor market conditions or when we can't easily convert the assets to cash.\" The report does not disclose this risk. [4] ISA tax treatment change risk: The product literature [P7] states \"It's possible that the current favourable tax treatment of Individual Savings Accounts may change in the future.\" The report does not disclose this risk to the client who is investing in an ISA. [5] Inflation risk: The product literature [P5] warns \"If investments grow less quickly than the rate of inflation, the buying power of your money will reduce.\" The report does not explicitly disclose this risk, although it does note cashflow assumptions. [6] Charge impact on growth: The product literature [P10, P16] emphasizes \"The price of some investments includes initial and yearly costs that will have an effect on your investments. Initial costs will have an immediate effect on the value of those investments\" and \"Depending on how funds take their charges has an impact on your investment growth or income. If charges are taken from your investment, its growth may be constrained.\" While the report discloses the adviser fee impact (1.08% annual outperformance required), it does not disclose the specific impact of fund charges on growth or the combined effect of all charges (platform + fund + adviser). [7] Disinvestment risks: The product literature [P10] describes the disinvestment process and associated risks when there is insufficient cash to pay income. The report does not disclose this risk. [8] Cancellation and market movement risk: The product literature [P5, P10] warns that if cancellation occurs within the cancellation period and the investment has fallen in value, the client may not recover the full original payment. The report does not disclose this specific risk. [9] Smooth Managed Funds ISA risk: The product literature [P7] contains a specific warning about Smooth Managed Funds: \"Any invalid investment into Smooth Managed Funds cannot be corrected or 'repaired' and all investments will be voided. It's important for you to note that if you breach the Regulations or legislation in respect of payments into the Smooth Managed Funds, the life insurance policy though which the funds are held will terminate and all of your investments made into the Smooth Managed Funds for all tax years will be void.\" The report does not disclose whether the recommended ISA investment involves Smooth Managed Funds or this specific risk.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Fund composition of recommended Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II (whether it contains emerging markets, small company, currency-exposed, or property investments)","Fund composition of recommended CT Universal MAP Cautious Fund C Acc for ISA (whether it contains emerging markets, small company, currency-exposed, or property investments)","Whether the recommended ISA investment involves Smooth Managed Funds","Specific features or guarantees being given up in the pension transfer from existing Aviva arrangement","Detailed breakdown of all charges (platform, fund, adviser) and their combined impact on projected growth","Whether the existing pension arrangement is a defined benefit or defined contribution scheme"],"analysis":"The report discloses some material disadvantages and risks, but the disclosure is incomplete and inconsistent with the supporting evidence. The report addresses access delays, adviser fee impact, market timing risk, critical yield, capital gains tax, capital risk, and cashflow limitations. However, the product literature contains numerous material risks that are either absent from the report or only partially disclosed. \n\nThe most significant gaps are: (1) Pension transfer risks — the product literature explicitly warns about loss of valuable rights in defined benefit schemes and provides no guarantee of better outcomes, but the report does not disclose what specific benefits or protections the client may be losing by transferring from the existing Aviva arrangement; (2) Asset class-specific risks — the product literature contains detailed warnings about emerging markets, small companies, currency exposure, and property investments, but the report does not identify whether the recommended funds contain these exposures or disclose these risks; (3) Fund suspension and dealing restrictions — a material risk disclosed in product literature but not in the report; (4) ISA tax treatment change risk — disclosed in product literature but not in the report; (5) Inflation risk — disclosed in product literature but not explicitly in the report; (6) Charge impact on growth — while the report discloses the adviser fee impact, it does not disclose the specific impact of fund charges or the combined effect of all charges on investment growth; (7) Disinvestment risks — disclosed in product literature but not in the report; (8) Cancellation and market movement risk — disclosed in product literature but not in the report; (9) Smooth Managed Funds ISA risk — disclosed in product literature but not addressed in the report.\n\nThe report's statement that \"All other risks associated with this investment are detailed in the product literature provided to you\" delegates risk disclosure to product literature rather than ensuring the report itself contains fair, clear and prominent disclosure of material risks. This is a disclosure shortfall. The report does not establish which risks in the product literature are material to this client's specific circumstances, and does not explain them in the report itself.\n\nThe critical yield disclosure (2.94% per year required to match current plan) is present but appears to conflict with the derived figures analysis, which notes that the recommended option costs 0.08% more annually yet shows no difference in projected outcomes at Low and Mid growth rates, and only a 0.1% difference at High growth. The report does not reconcile these inconsistencies or explain what assumptions underlie the critical yield calculation.\n\nThe adviser fee impact disclosure (1.08% annual outperformance required) is present and client-specific, but the report does not disclose the combined impact of platform charges (0.21% recommended vs 0.32% existing), fund charges (0.41% recommended vs 0.22% existing), and adviser fees (1% ongoing) on the total cost of the recommendation or the total outperformance required.\n\nThe report does not disclose that the client is giving up the existing Vanguard LifeStrategy funds (which provide a balanced, diversified approach with clear risk levels) for the Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II, or what specific features, protections or benefits may be lost in this transition.","citations":[{"passageId":"P9","source":"Aviva - Pension - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"If you're transferring benefits from an older company pension scheme it could be a Defined Benefit Pension and you may be losing valuable rights or benefits within the scheme such as a guaranteed income based on your final salary. As a result of the transfer you may not get higher benefits in the new scheme as the growth is defined by future investment returns and these and the retirement income from them can go down as well as up. You should consider all your options prior to transferring, to decide which is the best choice for you.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Aviva - Pension - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"There's no guarantee that what you receive at retirement will be greater than what you could have received from the previous scheme.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Aviva - Pension - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"Investments in emerging or developing markets may carry higher risks as they may be subject to considerable fluctuations in value. Small company investments may take longer to sell than those in larger companies, so the price fluctuations may be greater. The value of any overseas investments will be influenced by the rate of exchange used to convert to sterling.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Aviva - ISA - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"You may not be able to sell or switch between funds if the fund manager concerned has deferred or suspended dealing in the fund at that time. This is more likely to occur during times of poor market conditions or when we can't easily convert the assets to cash, such as where the fund invests directly or indirectly in land or buildings.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Aviva - ISA - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"It's possible that the current favourable tax treatment of Individual Savings Accounts may change in the future.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Aviva - ISA - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"If investments grow less quickly than the rate of inflation, the buying power of your money will reduce.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Aviva - Pension - Key Features.md","category":"H","quote":"The price of some investments includes initial and yearly costs that will have an effect on your investments. Initial costs will have an immediate effect on the value of those investments, so you should view such investments as medium to long term (typically more than five years).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Aviva - Pension - Key Features.md","